// The standard collections are not written for elements that own something.
//
// Nothing in `Vector<T>` mentions ownership, which is the point -- but three
// of the classes behind it move elements by reading a slot and storing what
// they read, and a handle read out of a slot is a second handle to one object.
// While the rules did not run inside a generic body that was invisible; now
// that each instantiation is judged, `Vector<Cell*>` says so in full.
//
// This is recorded as a test rather than fixed because what `iterate()` and
// `builder()` should mean for an element that owns something is a design
// decision and not a patch: a snapshot cannot own the elements it snapshots,
// and a builder filled from a live vector cannot own what that vector still
// holds. Both want a way to say "the subscriber form of `T`" -- `sub T` where
// `T` is already a complete type -- which the language cannot spell yet.
//
// `Vector<int32>` is unaffected, and the rest of the suite uses exactly that.
// The corresponding container written the way the model requires is
// tests/vector-owner-elements.abs.

import "../std/collections/vector.abs";

class Cell {
    public int32 value;
    public Cell(int32 initial) { value = initial; }
}

int32 main() {
    Vector<int32>* counted = new Vector<int32>();
    counted.push(1);
    delete counted;

    Vector<Cell*>* owning = new Vector<Cell*>();
    delete owning;
    return 0;
}
